From: V <61495413+kelo221@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 06:07:21 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Ay::loadFile: refuse chip clocks above 4 MHz

The chip does work for every tick of its clock, and Ay took any clock: at
4294967295 Hz a voice took about seven times real time to render, on the
audio thread. SoLoud's dump tools write 1774400 (ZX Spectrum) and 2000000
(Atari ST), and the fastest of these chips is rated for a 4 MHz input
clock (the YM2149, with its divider); a dump's chip clock is now at most
4000000 Hz.
---
 src/audiosource/ay/soloud_ay.cpp | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/audiosource/ay/soloud_ay.cpp b/src/audiosource/ay/soloud_ay.cpp
index 6bc8ea1..9f495c9 100644
--- a/src/audiosource/ay/soloud_ay.cpp
+++ b/src/audiosource/ay/soloud_ay.cpp
@@ -171,6 +171,12 @@ namespace SoLoud
 		// a sample a tick, or one tick could overrun the buffer.
 		if ((unsigned int)chipspeed < 8 * 44100 || (long long)(unsigned int)cpuspeed * (8192 - 20) / 44100 / 2 == 0)
 			return FILE_LOAD_FAILED;
+		// The chip works on every tick of its clock, which any speed made
+		// unbounded. SoLoud's dump tools write 1774400 (ZX Spectrum) and
+		// 2000000 (Atari ST); the fastest of these chips takes a 4 MHz input
+		// clock (the YM2149 with its divider).
+		if ((unsigned int)chipspeed > 4000000)
+			return FILE_LOAD_FAILED;
 		// Zeroed: an odd last byte makes an op with a high byte of 0.
 		unsigned short *ops = new unsigned short[(length + 1) / 2]();
 		aFile->seek(dataofs);
